      "title": "Farmers and agribusinesses test AI to cut costs and improve crops",
      "content_html": "Agricultural companies, researchers and farmers are using artificial intelligence for crop monitoring, breeding, irrigation and farm administration, as tools move from research into field trials. A survey previewed at the PLUSAGRI 2026 conference in Warsaw on Nov. 18 found that 53.9% of participating Polish farmers said they use AI; organizers planned to present findings on how farmers use the technology and how much they trust it. Sweden’s Lantmännen says it uses AI in plant breeding and to improve the use of fertilizer, crop protection products and water.\n\nApplications range from software that analyzes crop images and farm data to AI-guided irrigation. A European potato program supported by Cropin examined how farming practices affect water use and potato quality, while researchers have reported AI systems that identify crop models from agricultural data. A Chinese provincial study analyzed data from 2005 to 2023, while newer tools still require testing across crops and climates before broader use.",
