      "title": "Bobby Ray Inman",
      "content_html": "<p><span class=\"autocomment\">Nomination for Secretary of Defense</span></p>\n<table style=\"background-color:#fff;color:#202122;\">\n<tr class=\"diff-title\" lang=\"en\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#fff;color:#202122;text-align:center;\">← Previous revision</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color:#fff;color:#202122;text-align:center;\">Revision as of 06:00, 20 November 2026</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td class=\"diff-lineno\" colspan=\"2\">Line 86:</td>\n<td class=\"diff-lineno\" colspan=\"2\">Line 86:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>In 1994, after Inman asked to be withdrawn from consideration as Defense Secretary, his critics speculated that the decision was motivated by a desire to conceal his links to [[International Signal and Control|International Signal and Control (ISC)]]. Inman was a member of the board of directors of the company, which allegedly either was negligent or approved illegal exports.&lt;ref&gt;[http://richardknight.homestead.com/inman.html Bobby Ray Inman and South Africa]&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>In 1994, after Inman asked to be withdrawn from consideration as Defense Secretary, his critics speculated that the decision was motivated by a desire to conceal his links to [[International Signal and Control|International Signal and Control (ISC)]]. Inman was a member of the board of directors of the company, which allegedly either was negligent or approved illegal exports.&lt;ref&gt;[http://richardknight.homestead.com/inman.html Bobby Ray Inman and South Africa]&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br/></td>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br/></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#ffe49c;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>Originally called ESI (Electronic Systems International), the company manufactured sub-assemblies for the [[AGM-45 Shrike]] and [[RIM-7 Sea Sparrow]] missiles in 1974, and just after the [[Vietnam War]] which was part of a standard arms contract for the <del style=\"font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;\">US</del> defense administration (DCAS). The company also had a commercial repair facility of two meter portable [[Amateur radio|amateur (\"ham\") radios]] from a company in New Jersey called Clegg,&lt;ref&gt;{{Cite web|url=http://www.rigpix.com/clegg/fmdx.htm|title=RigPix Database – Clegg – FM-DX|website=www.rigpix.com|access-date=2017-01-30}}&lt;/ref&gt; and manufactured communications helmet radios for firemen, and electronic outdoor <del style=\"font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;\">[[</del>bug <del style=\"font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;\">zappers]]±+±)9</del>.</div></td>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#a3d3ff;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>Originally called ESI (Electronic Systems International), the company manufactured sub-assemblies for the [[AGM-45 Shrike]] and [[RIM-7 Sea Sparrow]] missiles in 1974, and just after the [[Vietnam War]] which was part of a standard arms contract for the <ins style=\"font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;\">U.S.</ins> defense administration (DCAS). The company also had a commercial repair facility of two meter portable [[Amateur radio|amateur (\"ham\") radios]] from a company in New Jersey called Clegg,&lt;ref&gt;{{Cite web|url=http://www.rigpix.com/clegg/fmdx.htm|title=RigPix Database – Clegg – FM-DX|website=www.rigpix.com|access-date=2017-01-30}}&lt;/ref&gt; and manufactured communications helmet radios for firemen, and electronic outdoor bug <ins style=\"font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;\">zapperd</ins>.</div></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br/></td>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br/></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>ISC was involved in two major indiscretions, for which CEO James Guerin received ¡1+a 15-year prison sentence:</div></td>\n<td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n<td style=\"background-color:#f8f9fa;color:#202122;font-size:88%;border-style:solid;border-width:1px 1px 1px 4px;border-radius:0.33em;border-color:#eaecf0;vertical-align:top;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><div>ISC was involved in two major indiscretions, for which CEO James Guerin received ¡1+a 15-year prison sentence:</div></td>\n</tr>\n</table>",
